.TH PRIMESTEST 1
.SH NAME
primestest \- regression test for primes(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B primestest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Primestest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/primes.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR runstring(args) ,
and checks a bounded
.RI ( start
.IR finish )
range against golden output for each of several cases: a range within
the pre-listed small-primes table, a range entirely past it (one sieve
segment), a range straddling the table's own boundary, equal
.IR start / finish
(both prime and both composite),
.I finish
below
.IR start ,
and a
.I start
of 0.
.PP
.IR Primes (1)
is a translation of Plan 9's own
.B sys/src/cmd/primes.c
rather than a workalike, so its real oracle is the algorithm's own
unambiguous mathematical definition (every expected value here was
checked independently, not derived from the sieve's own logic), cross-
checked separately during development against the real
.I primes
binary (this tree's local
.B plan9port
checkout) for ranges where their command-line forms align.
.PP
Prints one line per failure to standard error, a final
.IR N " passed, " N " failed"
summary to standard output, and exits with a non-nil status if
anything failed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/primestest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR primes (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I primes
in-process via its
.B runstring
seam, not as a spawned subprocess reading real standard input - so it
does not exercise
.IR primes 's
own
.B init
or its zero-argument (read
.I start
from standard input) form at all. Nor does it test the unbounded
(one-argument, no
.IR finish )
form, since that runs indefinitely by design and would never return
from an in-process call; that form was checked manually against the
real oracle during development instead.
